course: dictionary
lesson: 7188
title: Persuasion
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Persuasion means getting someone to believe or do something by giving good reasons. «Over 200 children play here every week» is persuasion, because it gives the council a good reason to keep the park open.
explain: Persuasion uses reasons, evidence and feelings, and a polite voice works best. «Dear Council, the park is boss, don't close it or else!» sounds more like a threat than a polite request, even though boss means great here. The threat is the clue that this is pushy rather than fair.
explain: Ask yourself: what do they want me to feel? A balanced argument gives the reasons on both sides, such as for and against banning school uniforms, before the writer says what they think at the end.
fact: Persuasion means getting someone to believe or do something by giving good reasons.
fact: «Persuasion» can also mean the skill of doing this, as in «powers of persuasion».
fact: A persuasion, in a formal sense, is a set of beliefs, such as a religious or political group: «people of all persuasions».
fact: «With a little persuasion» means someone was gently talked round to agree.
fact: Adverts, strangers and scams online may use persuasion, so always ask what they want you to feel or do.
exercise: «Over 200 children play here every week.» Why is this persuasive? | Because it asks about the weather | Because it is a funny joke | *Because it gives the council a good reason to keep the park open | Because it is a threat
exercise: «Dear Council, the park is boss, don't close it or else!» What does boss mean here? | Awful and dull | Closed for repairs | Dirty and wet | *Great
exercise: In «people of all persuasions», what does persuasion mean? | *A set of beliefs, such as a religious or political group | Getting someone to buy a car | A loud argument about school | A kind of dance
test: Persuasion means getting someone to believe or do something by doing what? | *Giving good reasons | Ignoring them completely | Shouting loudly | Making them fall asleep
test: In «with a little persuasion», what does the phrase suggest? | They ran away from the idea | They were forced to do it by a big threat | They never agreed at all | *They were gently talked round to agree
exam: Which is the best way to check whether a text is trying to persuade you? | Read it backwards | Read only the first line | Count the letters in each word | *Ask what they want you to feel, and look for reasons and evidence
